<StatePage title="Conditional Lien Waiver: Payment, Scope & State Forms" description="Review conditional versus unconditional waivers, progress and final payments, reserved claims, and statutory forms in California, Texas, Florida, and Michigan." canonical="https://mechanicslienform.com/lien-waiver/conditional/"><article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 prose prose-lg"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/mechanics-lien/">Document guides</a></nav>
<h1>Conditional lien waivers: match the release to the payment</h1><p>A conditional waiver makes its effect depend on the payment condition stated in the document. An unconditional waiver generally releases the identified rights without that protection. Read the actual language and applicable state law rather than treating the title as a guarantee.</p>
<h2>Progress payment and final payment are different events</h2><p>A progress waiver commonly addresses work through a specified date while preserving later work and identified exceptions. A final waiver can close a much wider group of claims. Compare the through-date with the payment application and invoice schedule; it may reach work beyond the invoice being paid.</p><p>For example, a payment application may include unpaid retention and disputed changes even though the incoming check covers only an approved base amount. Check how the applicable statutory form treats those items and document any permitted exceptions. Do not assume a handwritten reservation is valid in every state.</p>
<h2>Use the applicable statutory forms</h2><ul><li><strong>California:</strong> Civil Code sections 8132–8138 provide four forms covering conditional/unconditional progress and final releases. Their scope includes other construction remedies as stated in the forms. Use the applicable statutory text.</li><li><strong>Texas:</strong> Property Code section 53.284 supplies four statutory forms. Review section 53.281 and the contract-date rules; avoid a generic substitute for a required statutory form.</li><li><strong>Florida:</strong> Section 713.20 supplies progress and final forms, permits conditioning a check-based waiver on payment, and addresses alternate language. Florida does have statutory forms.</li><li><strong>Michigan:</strong> MCL 570.1115 supplies partial/full conditional and unconditional forms. Check the applicable statutory form and payment condition. Michigan also has statutory forms.</li></ul>
<h2>A practical review before signing</h2><ol><li>Match the claimant, customer, owner, project, and property.</li><li>Identify whether the payment is progress or final and whether it has cleared.</li><li>Compare the released amount and through-date with the ledger.</li><li>Check retention, disputed changes, bond claims, and other rights within the document's scope.</li><li>Use permitted exceptions and obtain review before altering statutory language.</li><li>Keep the executed waiver, payment evidence, and corresponding payment application together.</li></ol><p>A check in hand and collected funds are different facts. Have your accounting process record when payment clears. If the document is unconditional, obtain advice before signing while payment remains pending. If a condition fails, preserve bank records and the exact signed wording.</p>
<h2>Keep waiver review separate from lien deadlines</h2><p>Payment negotiations and waiver exchanges do not necessarily extend notice or recording deadlines. Review the property-state page for <a href="/mechanics-lien/illinois/">Illinois</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/new-york/">New York</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/florida/">Florida</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/north-carolina/">North Carolina</a>, or <a href="/mechanics-lien/colorado/">Colorado</a>. Other states are in the <a href="/mechanics-lien/">directory</a>.</p>
<h2>A waiver and discharge do different jobs</h2><p>If a claim is already recorded or filed, determine whether a separate <a href="/mechanics-lien/release/">release or discharge</a> must identify and clear that claim from the records. A payment waiver should not be assumed to complete the recording-office or court process. Use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">claim checklist</a> to locate the original instrument details.</p>
